#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec9ea01-bfeb-5dfa-bb0d-e8dda89e8cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281819} {:answer/id #uuid "2f167cc3-d0d4-5732-aea7-9737587d5446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282107} {:answer/id #uuid "a46851a8-a830-5300-ba0a-c94605be1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283122} {:answer/id #uuid "a598ce3d-2796-5960-afd3-75d843fcccbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c751ad-dde6-5f48-96a4-ca006c0a37ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a598ce3d-2796-5960-afd3-75d843fcccbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} :answer-field/id #uuid "f183aa4f-94f5-53a7-8fa6-3240e8f8d306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47feb8ed-9de6-5cab-9530-5d8b3448a054" :question/math-academy-id "q-165413" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d386b2-29ca-55a0-a4f9-2285709e1710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342276} {:answer/id #uuid "0724c421-8073-517d-93f3-1620e786e609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342301} {:answer/id #uuid "39b44baa-ac93-5399-be44-580cc1caf867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342714} {:answer/id #uuid "5e406d1b-7ff9-563d-ad72-bf188c813dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343029} {:answer/id #uuid "e4798884-9668-5922-995d-443c2be3e374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0724c421-8073-517d-93f3-1620e786e609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342301} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ab5eb9-1fc8-5190-8bd2-5881ace73b08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aefe264e-7dab-56be-82be-12f2dc01af9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76345" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496747} {:answer/id #uuid "3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496784} {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497127} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf0e6db-e5f4-547e-8dad-d9a52bf566a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76608" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x(1-{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b1571f-f5f7-562d-80a0-607aa3516530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342403} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9c1e54-997f-58a0-bd84-3b903059ab31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3-{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342870} {:answer/id #uuid "7da69662-1fe5-58e3-b0c0-5897eb5c94d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd26a22-60c5-5518-ab97-c388fe07fef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344065} {:answer/id #uuid "d1097377-be4e-5b0d-8118-afb24f1f0f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da69662-1fe5-58e3-b0c0-5897eb5c94d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5e348e-6403-58e5-a33c-30c8b85d2e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d123bbf4-7b8c-5b08-b206-9ffde691a39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76610" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-2{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cee7c2d-32de-5ecb-b754-ad79568f9d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342741} {:answer/id #uuid "5fee99df-a2f2-53ad-aa42-aeb2b8227900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343052} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b8c0d-eb50-5a20-b648-4799432e59b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343321} {:answer/id #uuid "817d31a5-4314-5a33-8e83-382ed5fd5388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343350} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7f6c1c-7a72-538c-9708-f7cf3bcb5c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d7f6c1c-7a72-538c-9708-f7cf3bcb5c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343475} :answer-field/id #uuid "946d4e0c-fe04-5d7a-bb82-af9edb848223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a681b3bc-0951-5b58-83e9-55d23d5a0373" :question/math-academy-id "q-76688" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
